WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.830
Four square tiles, each with side one,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.470
are pushed together to make a two by two square.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.950
What is the perimeter of the large square?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.330
We will find it by counting tile edges.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.380
Separately, each tile has four edges.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.060
Four tiles report sixteen edges altogether.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.670
When the tiles are joined, some edges end up inside.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.380
There are four inside joins: two along the vertical

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.210
middle line and two along the horizontal middle line.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.640
Each inside join was reported by both tiles beside it.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.460
So remove two reports for each of the four joins.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.740
Sixteen minus eight is eight.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:40.520
The perimeter is eight.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.300
Check directly: the big square has side two,

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.280
and four times two is eight.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.000
Now rearrange the tiles in a single row.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.470
There are only three joins, so the perimeter is sixteen

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.320
minus six, which is ten.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.700
Fewer joins means a longer perimeter.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.360
The outside perimeter is the total of all tile edges,

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.200
minus two for every join.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.730
Rebuild this argument, then find the perimeter of six

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.700
unit squares arranged in a two by three rectangle.
